  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Veterans Affairs has obligated $68.4B in contracts in FY2026 to date, across 58,673 awards with an average award size of $1.2M. Obligations fell 12.6% year over year. Contract spending has grown since FY21, moving from $47.7B in FY21 to $78.3B in FY25, then to $68.4B in FY26 to date. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers account for the largest share of current-year obligations at $28.6B, followed by Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $11.1B and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) at $7.6B. Health-related services, medical products, and IT services show up across the top NAICS categories, with Computer Systems Design Services at $3.2B and Other Computer Related Services at $2B. OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. is the top vendor with $19.9B in obligations, followed by MCKESSON CORPORATION with $10.8B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP with $9B. The current-year totals are partial and reported on a fiscal year to date basis.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How are obligations changing year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $68.4B in FY2026 to date and obligations fell 12.6% year over year. Obligations trended upward from $47.7B in FY21 to $78.3B in FY25 before moving to $68.4B in FY26 to date. The FY26 total is a fiscal year to date figure, so year comparisons reflect partial current-year data."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where does Veterans Affairs concentrate procurement spending?",
        "body": "Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest NAICS category at $28.6B, followed by Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $11.1B and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) at $7.6B. IT services appear among the top categories with Computer Systems Design Services at $3.2B and Other Computer Related Services at $2B. Medical products and supply categories include Medical, Dental, and Hospital Equipment and Supplies Merchant Wholesalers at $912M and Surgical and Medical Instrument Manufacturing at $691M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Veterans Affairs spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs has obligated $68.4B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency recorded 58,673 awards with an average award size of $1.2M, and obligations fell 12.6% year over year. The FY26 figure is a fiscal year to date total and current-year totals are partial."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Veterans Affairs buy?",
      "a": "Veterans Affairs buys health services, medical products, and IT and construction services. Top NAICS categories in FY2026 to date are Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $28.6B, Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $11.1B, and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) at $7.6B. IT and construction-related categories include Computer Systems Design Services at $3.2B, Other Computer Related Services at $2B, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $2.4B."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Veterans Affairs's largest contractors?",
      "a": "The largest contractors for Veterans Affairs in FY2026 to date are OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. at $19.9B, MCKESSON CORPORATION at $10.8B, and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $9B. Other top vendors include QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $2.2B, OPTUMSERVE HEALTH SERVICES, INC. at $1.8B, and LOYAL SOURCE GOVERNMENT SERVICES LLC at $1.8B."
